  <article class="container-prose pt-12 pb-16">

    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Investment Mortgage: A Complete Guide</h1>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8">Investment mortgages follow different rules than the home loan most people are familiar with. Lenders treat income-generating properties as higher-risk, which means higher rates, larger down payments, and a more scrutinized application. But the qualification path depends heavily on which loan type you use — and the options have expanded considerably over the past decade.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">This guide covers how investment property financing works: the loan types available, how lenders think about risk, what you need to qualify, and how to choose the right structure for your situation.</p>

    <nav class="mt-10 p-6 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-xl">
      <p class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-3">Table of Contents</p>
      <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]">
        <li><a href="#what-is-an-investment-mortgage" class="hover:underline">What is an Investment Mortgage?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#same-as-regular" class="hover:underline">Is an Investment Mortgage the Same as a Regular Mortgage?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-is-an-investment-property-loan" class="hover:underline">What is an Investment Property Loan?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#types-of-loans" class="hover:underline">Types of Loans for an Investment Property</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dscr-loan" class="hover:underline">DSCR Loan for Investment Property</a></li>
        <li><a href="#types-of-investment-properties" class="hover:underline">Types Of Investment Properties</a></li>
        <li><a href="#requirements" class="hover:underline">Investment Property Loan Requirements and Qualifications</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-to-qualify" class="hover:underline">How to Qualify for an Investment Property Loan</a></li>
        <li><a href="#less-than-20" class="hover:underline">Can I Put Less than 20% Down on an Investment Property?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#minimum-down-payment" class="hover:underline">What is the Minimum Down Payment for an Investment Property?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#fha-loan" class="hover:underline">Can You Use an FHA Loan for an Investment Property?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#example" class="hover:underline">What is an Example of an Investment Loan?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-type-of-mortgage" class="hover:underline">What Type of Mortgage Should I Get for an Investment Property?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#conclusion" class="hover:underline">Conclusion</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="what-is-an-investment-mortgage" class="mt-12">What is an Investment Mortgage?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">An investment mortgage is a loan for a property you buy to generate income: a rental house, a duplex, a unit you plan to renovate and sell, or a property held for long-term appreciation. The underlying structure is the same as any mortgage, but the pricing and requirements reflect the fact that lenders hold investment properties to a higher standard than primary residences.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">At Cliffco, we originate several types of investment property loans: conventional loans for qualified borrowers buying standard rentals, DSCR loans that qualify on the property's cash flow rather than your personal income, Non-QM programs for borrowers with complex income, and renovation loans that roll purchase and repair costs into one note. The right one depends on your financial profile and what you're buying.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Investment property mortgage rates reflect the lender's read on two things: your creditworthiness, and the property's potential to generate stable income. Both matter, though the weight each gets varies by loan program.</p>

    <h2 id="same-as-regular" class="mt-12">Is an Investment Mortgage the Same as a Regular Mortgage?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">No. The mechanics are similar, but the numbers are different. Investment property loans typically carry rates 0.5% to 0.75% above what you'd see on a primary residence loan. Down payments run higher: at least 15% for single-family rentals, 25% for multi-family. Credit score minimums start at 680, with the best pricing reserved for 740 and above. Debt-to-income requirements are tighter too.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The reason is straightforward: lenders know from decades of data that borrowers are more likely to prioritize keeping their primary home over an investment property when finances get tight. That risk is priced in from the start.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Credit and income documentation requirements also differ. Conventional investment loans want W-2s, pay stubs, and two years of tax returns. DSCR loans skip personal income verification entirely. Non-QM programs sit in between, often using bank statements or a profit and loss statement rather than tax returns.</p>

    <h2 id="what-is-an-investment-property-loan" class="mt-12">What is an Investment Property Loan?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Investment property loans are a category, not a single product. Each program has its own documentation requirements, qualifying logic, and ideal borrower profile. Your credit score, down payment, income type, and the property itself all affect which programs you can access — and lenders weigh those factors differently depending on the program.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The range of available programs has expanded significantly over the past decade. DSCR loans, bank statement programs, and Non-QM products have opened up real estate investing to borrowers who wouldn't have qualified under conventional-only guidelines. That's a meaningful shift for investors with strong cash flow but complex income documentation.</p>

    <h2 id="types-of-loans" class="mt-12">Types of Loans for an Investment Property</h2>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Conventional loans</strong> require strong credit (680 minimum, 740+ for the best rates), standard income documentation, and down payments of 15% to 25%. They offer the most competitive rates for well-qualified borrowers and work well for straightforward single-family rentals.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>DSCR loans</strong> qualify based on the property's rental income rather than your personal income. If the monthly rent covers the mortgage payment, you can likely qualify — no tax returns required, no employment verification. This makes DSCR the go-to program for investors building portfolios or those with complex personal income.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Non-QM loans</strong> cover the range of cases that don't fit conventional criteria: self-employed borrowers documenting income through bank statements, investors with multiple LLCs, and borrowers whose tax returns don't reflect their actual cash flow.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Renovation loans</strong> roll acquisition and improvement costs into a single note. The loan amount is based on the after-repair value rather than the purchase price, making them useful for fix-and-flip investors or value-add rental plays.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>FHA loans</strong> are technically for owner-occupied properties, but multi-family buyers (2-4 units) can use FHA financing when they occupy one unit. The main advantage: down payments as low as 3.5%.</p>

    <h2 id="dscr-loan" class="mt-12">DSCR Loan for Investment Property</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">DSCR stands for Debt Service Coverage Ratio. It measures whether a property's rental income is sufficient to cover its mortgage payment.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>DSCR = Monthly Gross Rent ÷ Monthly PITIA</strong> (principal, interest, taxes, insurance, and HOA dues)</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Most lenders require a DSCR of at least 1.0 (rent equals the payment) to 1.25 (rent exceeds the payment by 25%). Properties that come in below 1.0 may still be approved at a higher rate or with a larger down payment, depending on the lender and the rest of the file.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">What makes DSCR loans useful for investors: there is no personal income verification. You don't need two years of tax returns, and being self-employed doesn't count against you. The property either pencils out or it doesn't. For borrowers building a portfolio, that matters — conventional DTI limits can cap how many properties you can finance, while DSCR loans sidestep that constraint entirely.</p>

    <h2 id="types-of-investment-properties" class="mt-12">Types Of Investment Properties</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">The property type affects which loans you can access and how the numbers work.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Single-family rentals</strong> are the most straightforward. Conventional financing is available, underwriting is familiar, and the rental market for houses is strong in most regions. They're a common starting point for new investors.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Multi-family properties</strong> (2-4 units) push DSCR numbers higher because multiple rent payments cover a single mortgage. They're more management-intensive, but the income density is better. They also open up FHA financing as an option when you occupy one unit.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">For both property types, the loan program that fits depends on how you earn and how you're documenting it — not just the property itself.</p>

    <h2 id="requirements" class="mt-12">Investment Property Loan Requirements and Qualifications</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">A 680 credit score gets you in the door for most conventional investment loans. A 720+ gets you meaningfully better rates. Most lenders want your DTI below 43%, even with projected rental income counted in your favor.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Reserves are a hard requirement. Most lenders want 6 to 12 months of mortgage payments held in liquid assets, documented through bank statements or investment accounts. If you own other financed properties, expect reserve requirements for those too.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Income documentation varies by loan type. Conventional loans want W-2s, recent pay stubs, and two years of tax returns. DSCR loans skip personal income entirely — the rent is the qualification. Non-QM bank statement programs calculate income from 12 to 24 months of deposits rather than tax returns. The program that fits depends on how you earn.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Loan limits vary by property type and location. And before closing, it's worth talking to a CPA about the tax treatment of rental income and expenses — mortgage interest, depreciation, and maintenance costs can all affect your net return meaningfully.</p>

    <h2 id="how-to-qualify" class="mt-12">How to Qualify for an Investment Property Loan</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Start with your credit profile: review your report for errors, pay down revolving balances, and avoid new credit inquiries in the months before applying. Build your reserves beyond the minimum — lenders want to see real liquidity, and coming in with six months rather than barely three makes a difference in how your file reads.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Get your documents organized before you start shopping:</p>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
      <li>Two years of personal and business tax returns</li>
      <li>Recent pay stubs or profit and loss statements</li>
      <li>Bank statements showing consistent income and savings</li>
      <li>Current lease agreements from existing rental properties</li>
      <li>Documentation of other income sources</li>
      <li>Asset statements showing reserve funds</li>
      <li>Current property insurance information</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">Know the market before you present a property to a lender. DSCR underwriting involves reviewing market rents, so having rent comps ready speeds the process. For conventional loans, the appraiser will prepare a rent schedule — their opinion of market rent is what the lender uses, not yours.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">If your first application doesn't clear, it's usually fixable. A larger down payment lowers the LTV. More reserves demonstrate stability. A better-performing property improves DSCR. Work with the lender to identify the specific gap before assuming you're out.</p>

    <h2 id="less-than-20" class="mt-12">Can I Put Less than 20% Down on an Investment Property?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Yes, in specific situations. FHA loans on 2-4 unit properties with owner occupancy allow as little as 3.5% down. Some Non-QM and portfolio lenders offer programs at 10% to 15% down for strong borrowers, usually at a rate premium. Conventional investment property loans start at 15% down for single-family properties.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The tradeoff is straightforward: a smaller down payment means a higher loan balance, a higher rate, and a less favorable DSCR. For investors who can afford more down, putting in more typically pays off in the rate and terms they receive.</p>

    <h2 id="minimum-down-payment" class="mt-12">What is the Minimum Down Payment for an Investment Property?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">For conventional investment loans: 15% for single-family, 25% for multi-family. Non-QM and portfolio lenders sometimes consider 10% for well-qualified borrowers, offset by a higher rate. FHA multi-family with owner occupancy allows 3.5% down.</p>

    <h2 id="fha-loan" class="mt-12">Can You Use an FHA Loan for an Investment Property?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">FHA loans are for primary residences, but multi-family properties with 2 to 4 units qualify when you occupy one unit. That's the house hacking model: live in one unit, rent the others, and use the rental income to offset your payment. Down payments as low as 3.5%, with more flexible credit thresholds than conventional.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The catch is the owner-occupancy requirement — you need to live there for at least a year. It's a real commitment, but it's one of the few ways to enter real estate investing with a small down payment and favorable loan terms.</p>

    <h2 id="example" class="mt-12">What is an Example of an Investment Loan?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">A single-family rental priced at $300,000 with 20% down ($60,000) and a 30-year fixed rate at 6.5% produces a principal and interest payment of about $1,517 per month.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">For a DSCR example: a duplex priced at $400,000 where each unit rents for $2,000 generates $4,000 in gross monthly rent. If the PITIA on the loan is $2,800, the DSCR is 1.43 — well above the typical 1.0 to 1.25 minimum. The property qualifies based on its income alone, with no review of the borrower's personal income or employment history.</p>

    <h2 id="what-type-of-mortgage" class="mt-12">What Type of Mortgage Should I Get for an Investment Property?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">It comes down to your financial profile and the property.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Conventional loans</strong> offer the lowest rates for borrowers with strong credit and documented W-2 income. The tradeoff is strict documentation requirements and DTI limits that can cap how many properties you can carry before you're frozen out.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>DSCR loans</strong> are the best fit for investors who want to keep their personal income out of the qualification equation — or who are building a portfolio and don't want each new property depending on their personal DTI. The property's cash flow is the story, not your tax return.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Non-QM loans</strong> cover the cases in between: self-employed borrowers, bank statement income, multiple entities, complex tax returns. If conventional doesn't work and DSCR doesn't fit the property, Non-QM is usually where the path forward lives.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Renovation loans</strong> are purpose-built for value-add plays. If the property needs work before it can be rented or sold, rolling the purchase and renovation into one loan simplifies the process and bases the loan amount on what the property will be worth after repairs — not what you're paying for it today.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><strong>FHA multi-family</strong> is a starting point, not a scaling tool. If you want to live in one unit of a duplex while building equity, it works. Once you're building a portfolio, you'll move to conventional or DSCR.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Consider:</p>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
      <li>Your intended hold period for the property</li>
      <li>Your tolerance for payment fluctuations</li>
      <li>Future portfolio expansion plans</li>
      <li>Available down payment and reserves</li>
      <li>Current and projected rental market conditions</li>
      <li>Your experience level in real estate investing</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="conclusion" class="mt-12">Conclusion</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Investment property financing isn't one-size-fits-all. The loan that makes sense for a first-time landlord buying a single-family rental looks nothing like the program that works for an experienced investor running a multi-unit portfolio through an LLC. Getting that match right from the start — rather than forcing the wrong product and running into a wall — is most of the work.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">At Cliffco, we work with investors at every stage: first rental, growing portfolio, complex income, cash-flow properties. If you're not sure which direction fits your situation, that's what a loan officer is for. Tell us what you're buying and how you earn, and we'll tell you which programs apply.</p>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
      <div>
        <p class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Ready to finance your investment property?</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Contact Cliffco Mortgage Bankers today to discuss your investment goals and find the right loan.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary shrink-0">Get started</a>
    </div>

  </article>
